WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.686 --> 00:00:21.935
Hey, Little Fox readers! I'm Jake. Yesterday in Mr. Fay's class we learned about the Louvre, one of the biggest art museums in the whole world.

2
00:00:21.936 --> 00:00:28.274
I love to draw, so I was pretty excited when I heard we'd be learning about an art museum in France.

3
00:00:28.275 --> 00:00:31.541
And I'm not the only one who gets excited about art.

4
00:00:31.542 --> 00:00:38.785
Mr. Fay told us that 8.5 million people from all over the world visit the Louvre each year.

5
00:00:38.786 --> 00:00:42.449
Now that's what I call a lot of visitors!

6
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:47.437
The Louvre is located in the heart of Paris, on the right bank of the Seine River.

7
00:00:47.438 --> 00:00:53.928
It's home to around 35,000 pieces of art and nearly 13 kilometers of galleries.

8
00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:59.514
That means it would take almost a year for a visitor to see everything inside the museum!

9
00:00:59.515 --> 00:01:07.228
When the Louvre was first built, way back in 1190, it was designed to be a fortress to protect Paris from its enemies.

10
00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:14.109
At the time Paris was the largest city in Europe, and over the next two hundred years, the city continued to grow.

11
00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.161
Eventually the Louvre wasn't big enough to protect the entire city.

12
00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:24.635
So, in 1364, King Charles V decided to turn it into a palace.

13
00:01:24.636 --> 00:01:36.410
During the centuries that followed, members of the French royalty continued changing the Louvre, demolishing some sections and adding new buildings and wings, along with gardens and fancy decorations.

14
00:01:36.411 --> 00:01:45.539
French monarchs also collected artwork, filling the Louvre with colorful tapestries, elaborate carvings, and all kinds of incredible paintings.

15
00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:51.713
Eventually the royal court decided to move to a new palace, abandoning the Louvre for a while.

16
00:01:51.714 --> 00:01:59.363
Finally, in 1793, government officials decided to turn the buildings into a museum for the public.

17
00:01:59.364 --> 00:02:02.203
Today the Louvre has three main wings.

18
00:02:02.204 --> 00:02:08.858
They're arranged like a giant horseshoe, and in the courtyard in the middle of the wings is a big glass pyramid.

19
00:02:08.859 --> 00:02:13.522
Inside the Louvre, visitors can see art from many different time periods.

20
00:02:13.523 --> 00:02:24.518
There's a helmet worn by a real Roman gladiator; Egyptian statues over 4000 years old; and a sword used at the coronations of French kings.

21
00:02:24.519 --> 00:02:28.586
Mr. Fay also showed us some pictures of mummies at the museum.

22
00:02:28.587 --> 00:02:33.660
Melissa said they looked really creepy, but I thought they looked really cool!

23
00:02:33.661 --> 00:02:39.667
Most people visiting the Louvre stop to see the most famous painting in the world—the Mona Lisa.

24
00:02:39.668 --> 00:02:44.984
It's a small portrait of a woman, painted by Leonardo da Vinci in 1506.

25
00:02:44.985 --> 00:02:51.909
One reason the painting is famous is because da Vinci's brushwork makes the background look very realistic.

26
00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:58.591
But the main reason for the Mona Lisa's popularity is probably the mysterious smile on the woman's face.

27
00:02:58.592 --> 00:03:04.640
A lot of people think she's smiling because she has a secret. Hmm . . . What do you think?

28
00:03:04.641 --> 00:03:13.745
On August 21, 1911, a former worker at the Louvre, Vincenzo Peruggia, actually managed to steal the Mona Lisa.

29
00:03:13.746 --> 00:03:17.464
Peruggia went to the museum one day, pretending to be a visitor.

30
00:03:17.465 --> 00:03:21.998
When the museum closed for the night, he hid and slept there overnight.

31
00:03:21.999 --> 00:03:28.616
The next morning he simply removed the painting from the wall, concealed it under his clothing, and walked out the door!

32
00:03:28.617 --> 00:03:33.693
I guess they didn't have any high-tech security cameras or alarms back then.

33
00:03:33.694 --> 00:03:42.457
The Mona Lisa wasn't found until two years later, when Peruggia made the mistake of trying to sell it to an antiques dealer who reported him to the police.

34
00:03:42.458 --> 00:03:47.438
Ever since then the Mona Lisa has been kept behind some very strong glass.

35
00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:54.620
But it still attracts huge crowds, with thousands of visitors trying to catch a glimpse of the mysterious woman every day.

36
00:03:54.621 --> 00:04:00.436
The Raft of the Medusa by Théodore Géricault is another well-known painting at the Louvre.

37
00:04:00.437 --> 00:04:08.103
But this isn't one you could sneak out of a museum—it's almost five meters high and over seven meters wide!

38
00:04:08.104 --> 00:04:14.570
I found out that in 1939, the painting's enormous size caused an enormous problem.

39
00:04:14.571 --> 00:04:20.593
World War II had begun, and museum workers were worried about the valuable artwork inside the Louvre.

40
00:04:20.594 --> 00:04:25.510
So they decided to pack it up and move it to castles around France for safekeeping.

41
00:04:25.511 --> 00:04:29.427
Workers placed The Raft of the Medusa on the back of an open truck.

42
00:04:29.428 --> 00:04:34.669
On the drive out to the countryside, the tall painting knocked down some electrical wires.

43
00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:39.064
This caused the electricity to go out in an entire city!

44
00:04:39.065 --> 00:04:45.506
Another popular item at the Louvre is the Venus de Milo, a marble statue that was discovered by accident.

45
00:04:45.507 --> 00:04:52.777
In 1820 a farmer on the island of Melos was digging in the ground when he uncovered a statue in two pieces.

46
00:04:52.778 --> 00:04:58.592
The whole statue was carefully dug out, and then presented to King Louis XVIII of France.

47
00:04:58.593 --> 00:05:01.208
The king later donated it to the Louvre.

48
00:05:01.209 --> 00:05:06.736
Experts believe that the Venus de Milo was carved sometime around 100 BC.

49
00:05:06.737 --> 00:05:11.621
The statue is most likely of Aphrodite, the Greek goddess of love and beauty.

50
00:05:11.622 --> 00:05:18.049
But nobody knows this for sure because the statue is missing some parts, including its arms.

51
00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:22.300
Last night I went to the library and brought home a book about the Louvre.

52
00:05:22.301 --> 00:05:27.528
It has pictures of some of the artwork at the museum, and I practiced drawing a bunch of things.

53
00:05:27.529 --> 00:05:30.390
Oh, and I also created my own drawing.

54
00:05:30.391 --> 00:05:37.698
Check it out: it's a picture of Vincenzo Peruggia stealing the Mona Lisa while being chased by an Egyptian mummy!

55
00:05:37.699 --> 00:05:40.543
I hope you enjoyed learning more about the Louvre.

56
00:05:40.544 --> 00:05:44.341
See you again soon for another landmarks lesson!
